| principle 1, systems first | rule 7 |
| principle 2, no hidden runtime | rule 8 |
| principle 3, linearity and ownership | rule 9 |
| principle 4, second-class references | rule 10 |
| principle 5, visible capabilities and trust | rule 11 |
| principle 6, predictable execution | rule 16 |
| principle 7, core/shell separation | rule 16 |
| principle 8, artifact-first auditability | rule 14; conflict order item 2 |
| principle 9, proofs attach to real code | rule 7 |
| principle 10, small analyzable core | rule 15 |
| principle 11, honest trust boundaries | rule 12; conflict order item 1 |
| principle 12, compiler-owned identity | rule 13; conflict order item 1 |
| criterion 1, a simple invariant | AT 4 |
| criterion 2, more visible | AT 2 |
| criterion 3, less phase coupling | AT 5 |
| criterion 4, declaration-level dependencies | AT 5 |
| criterion 5, static or explicit dispatch | AT 6 |
| criterion 6, predictable code generation | AT 7 |
| criterion 7, clear diagnostics | AT 8 |
| criterion 8, one owning pass | AT 8 |
| criterion 9, the proof story | AT 4 |
| criterion 10, real benefit for audited code | AT 14 |
| criterion 11, crosses a contract boundary | AT 10 |
| criterion 12, fails visibly | AT 11 |
| criterion 13, evidence as precise as its claim | AT 12 |
| criterion 14, honest under hostile input | AT 13 |
| criterion 15, independent semantic identities | AT 12 |
| admission principle, quick decision rule, one-line test | AT, "In one line" |
| main rule for borrowed ideas | AT, "Borrowed ideas" |
| ordering principle | AT, "Ordering" |
| shape principles (features earn admission, removals improve, constraints compound, real programs test shape, record refusals) | rules 15, 17; AT, "Recording a refusal" |
| high-leverage priorities | not a design rule: the [roadmap](../ROADMAP.md) owns priority |
| ANTI_FEATURES.md entries and PROJECT.md's "no" and "not yet" decisions | [What Concrete refuses](#what-concrete-refuses); comparisons in [INFLUENCES.md](../legacy/docs/project/INFLUENCES.md#how-other-languages-answer-concretes-refusals) |
name = "concrete"
version = "0.2.0"
defaultTargets = ["ConcreteSpec"]

# The Lean Compiler Backend (LCB), pinned: slice 0's second backend. Its verified source semantics
# (LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source) is imported, never restated; core Lean only, no Mathlib.
[[require]]
name = "lean-compiler-backend"
git = "https://github.com/unbalancedparentheses/lean-compiler-backend"
rev = "4bea4b3ffd8a43a73a0016012d865044067b3275"

[leanOptions]
warningAsError = true

# The specification (ROADMAP A2, B): readable Lean with doc comments and conformance examples.
# Core Lean only; root ConcreteSpec.lean.
[[lean_lib]]
name = "ConcreteSpec"
leanOptions = { autoImplicit = false }

# The compiler (ROADMAP B0, slice 0; provisional): Lean code importing ConcreteSpec. Root Concrete.lean;
# design in Concrete/README.md.
[[lean_lib]]
name = "Concrete"
leanOptions = { autoImplicit = false }
/-!
# Basics: association lists

PROPOSED: not normative until the owner freezes Core (ROADMAP A2c). Part of `ConcreteSpec.Spec`;
see `ConcreteSpec/README.md`.

Every finite map in the specification (a context, an environment, a memory, a field list) is an
association list: a list of key–value pairs, read from the front. This module is the one
definition of the four operations on them; their lemmas are in `Proofs/Basics.lean`. No other
module defines its own lookup; a table of declarations, each carrying its own name (`findDecl`,
`Authority.Prog.find`), is searched with `List.find?`. `lookup`, `update` and `erase` take the
map first; `map` takes the function first, as `List.map` does. Call them qualified
(`AList.lookup Γ x`): on a plain `List` the dotted form would reach `List.erase` or `List.map`.
-/

namespace ConcreteSpec.Core

/-- A finite map as a list of key–value pairs. A key may occur more than once; the first entry
for a key is the one that counts. -/
abbrev AList (κ α : Type) := List (κ × α)

namespace AList

variable {κ α β : Type} [DecidableEq κ]

/-- The value of the first entry for `k`, if there is one. -/
def lookup : AList κ α → κ → Option α
  | [], _ => none
  | (k', a) :: l, k => if k' = k then some a else lookup l k

/-- Apply `f` to the value of the first entry for `k`. A map with no entry for `k` is unchanged. -/
def update : AList κ α → κ → (α → α) → AList κ α
  | [], _, _ => []
  | (k', a) :: l, k, f => if k' = k then (k', f a) :: l else (k', a) :: update l k f

/-- Remove every entry for `k`. -/
def erase : AList κ α → κ → AList κ α
  | [], _ => []
  | (k', a) :: l, k => if k' = k then erase l k else (k', a) :: erase l k

/-- Apply `f` to every value, keeping the keys. -/
def map (f : α → β) (l : AList κ α) : AList κ β := List.map (fun e => (e.1, f e.2)) l

/-! ### Examples -/

/-- The first entry for a key counts. -/
example : lookup [(1, 10), (2, 20), (1, 30)] 1 = some 10 := rfl

example : lookup [(1, 10), (2, 20)] 3 = none := rfl

/-- `update` changes the first entry only. -/
example : update [(1, 10), (2, 20), (1, 30)] 1 (· + 1) = [(1, 11), (2, 20), (1, 30)] := rfl

/-- `erase` removes every entry, so no older entry for the key shows through. -/
example : erase [(1, 10), (2, 20), (1, 30)] 1 = [(2, 20)] := rfl

example : map (· * 2) [(1, 10), (2, 20)] = [(1, 20), (2, 40)] := rfl

end AList

end ConcreteSpec.Core
/-!
# LL(1): a decidable check and its soundness

PROVISIONAL (slice 0). The grammar is always LL(1) (owner rule). This file gives a decision
procedure `ll1 : Grammar → Bool` and proves it sound against DECLARATIVE definitions of nullable,
FIRST, FOLLOW and prediction (`ll1_sound`): when `ll1 g = true`, no lookahead predicts two
different productions of one nonterminal, so a table-driven parser never has a choice to make.

The check is certificate-style: `compute` iterates to a fixed point, and `check` verifies that the
sets are CLOSED under the rules and that the prediction sets are disjoint. Soundness needs only
closure (a closed set contains the least one, which is the declarative relation), so the iteration
itself needs no proof; if it stops short, `check` fails and the grammar is refused.

The check is generic (any terminal and nonterminal types with decidable equality). Where the
language's grammar lives, and so where this file belongs, follows the spec's unification plan
(A2i): the grammar is spec data, and this file moves beside it when it lands.
-/

namespace Concrete.LL1

inductive Sym (T N : Type) where
  | t (a : T)
  | n (x : N)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

structure Grammar (T N : Type) where
  start : N
  prods : List (N × List (Sym T N))
  deriving Repr

variable {T N : Type} [DecidableEq T] [DecidableEq N]

/-! ## The declarative relations -/

section Decl
variable (g : Grammar T N)

/-- `x` derives the empty string. -/
inductive Nullable : N → Prop
  | intro {x : N} {xs : List N} :
      (x, xs.map Sym.n) ∈ g.prods → (∀ y ∈ xs, Nullable y) → Nullable x

/-- A sequence derives the empty string: it is all nonterminals, each nullable. -/
def NullableSeq (α : List (Sym T N)) : Prop :=
  ∃ xs : List N, α = xs.map Sym.n ∧ ∀ y ∈ xs, Nullable g y

/-- `a` can begin a string `x` derives. -/
inductive First : N → T → Prop
  | term {x : N} {pre post : List (Sym T N)} {a : T} :
      (x, pre ++ Sym.t a :: post) ∈ g.prods → NullableSeq g pre → First x a
  | nonterm {x y : N} {pre post : List (Sym T N)} {a : T} :
      (x, pre ++ Sym.n y :: post) ∈ g.prods → NullableSeq g pre → First y a → First x a

/-- `a` can begin a string the sequence `α` derives. -/
def FirstSeq (α : List (Sym T N)) (a : T) : Prop :=
  ∃ pre post, NullableSeq g pre ∧
    (α = pre ++ Sym.t a :: post ∨ ∃ y, α = pre ++ Sym.n y :: post ∧ First g y a)

/-- `b` can follow `x` (`none` is the end of input). This is the standard grammar-wide closure, not
reachability from the start symbol: unreachable productions contribute too, and FIRST does not ask
the rest of a form to be productive. Both over-approximate, so they can only refuse more grammars,
never hide a conflict. -/
inductive Follow : N → Option T → Prop
  | start : Follow g.start none
  | first {y x : N} {pre post : List (Sym T N)} {a : T} :
      (y, pre ++ Sym.n x :: post) ∈ g.prods → FirstSeq g post a → Follow x (some a)
  | tail {y x : N} {pre post : List (Sym T N)} {b : Option T} :
      (y, pre ++ Sym.n x :: post) ∈ g.prods → NullableSeq g post → Follow y b → Follow x b

/-- Production `i` is predicted on lookahead `b`. -/
def Predicts (i : Nat) (b : Option T) : Prop :=
  ∃ x rhs, g.prods[i]? = some (x, rhs) ∧
    ((∃ a, b = some a ∧ FirstSeq g rhs a) ∨ (NullableSeq g rhs ∧ Follow g x b))

end Decl

/-! ## The computed sets and the check -/

def symNullB (nu : List N) : Sym T N → Bool
  | .t _ => false
  | .n x => nu.contains x

def nullSeqB (nu : List N) (α : List (Sym T N)) : Bool := α.all (symNullB nu)

/-- The FIRST set of `x` in the table `fi`. -/
def firstOf (fi : List (N × T)) (x : N) : List T :=
  fi.filterMap fun (z, a) => if z = x then some a else none

def firstSeqB (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) : List (Sym T N) → List T
  | [] => []
  | .t a :: _ => [a]
  | .n y :: rest => firstOf fi y ++ (if nu.contains y then firstSeqB nu fi rest else [])

/-- Every way to split a sequence around one symbol. -/
def splits : List (Sym T N) → List (List (Sym T N) × Sym T N × List (Sym T N))
  | [] => []
  | s :: rest => ([], s, rest) :: (splits rest).map fun (p, s', q) => (s :: p, s', q)

def followOf (fo : List (N × Option T)) (x : N) : List (Option T) :=
  fo.filterMap fun (z, b) => if z = x then some b else none

def nuClosed (g : Grammar T N) (nu : List N) : Bool :=
  g.prods.all fun (x, rhs) => !nullSeqB nu rhs || nu.contains x

def fiClosed (g : Grammar T N) (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) : Bool :=
  g.prods.all fun (x, rhs) => (firstSeqB nu fi rhs).all fun a => fi.contains (x, a)

def foClosed (g : Grammar T N) (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) (fo : List (N × Option T)) :
    Bool :=
  fo.contains (g.start, none) &&
  g.prods.all fun (y, rhs) => (splits rhs).all fun (_, s, post) =>
    match s with
    | .t _ => true
    | .n x => (firstSeqB nu fi post).all (fun a => fo.contains (x, some a)) &&
        (!nullSeqB nu post || (followOf fo y).all fun b => fo.contains (x, b))

/-- The lookaheads that predict a production `x → rhs`. -/
def predictB (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) (fo : List (N × Option T)) (x : N)
    (rhs : List (Sym T N)) : List (Option T) :=
  (firstSeqB nu fi rhs).map some ++ (if nullSeqB nu rhs then followOf fo x else [])

def conflictFree (g : Grammar T N) (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) (fo : List (N × Option T)) :
    Bool :=
  (List.range g.prods.length).all fun i => (List.range g.prods.length).all fun j =>
    i == j || match g.prods[i]?, g.prods[j]? with
      | some (x, r), some (y, s) =>
        x != y || (predictB nu fi fo x r).all fun b => !(predictB nu fi fo y s).contains b
      | _, _ => true

/-- The check over given sets: closed, and the predictions of each nonterminal disjoint. -/
def check (g : Grammar T N) (nu : List N) (fi : List (N × T)) (fo : List (N × Option T)) : Bool :=
  nuClosed g nu && fiClosed g nu fi && foClosed g nu fi fo && conflictFree g nu fi fo

/-- Add `step s` to `s` until nothing new appears (or the fuel runs out). -/
def saturate {α : Type} [DecidableEq α] (step : List α → List α) : Nat → List α → List α
  | 0, s => s
  | fuel + 1, s =>
    let new := (step s).filter (fun a => !s.contains a) |>.eraseDups
    if new.isEmpty then s else saturate step fuel (s ++ new)

def terminals (g : Grammar T N) : List T :=
  (g.prods.flatMap fun (_, rhs) => rhs.filterMap fun | .t a => some a | .n _ => none).eraseDups

/-- The sets, by fixed-point iteration. The fuel bounds the number of pairs that can be added. -/
def compute (g : Grammar T N) : List N × List (N × T) × List (N × Option T) :=
  let k := g.prods.length + 1
  let fuel := k * (terminals g).length + 2 * k + 2
  let nu := saturate (fun nu => g.prods.filterMap fun (x, rhs) =>
    if nullSeqB nu rhs then some x else none) fuel []
  let fi := saturate (fun fi => g.prods.flatMap fun (x, rhs) =>
    (firstSeqB nu fi rhs).map fun a => (x, a)) fuel []
  let fo := saturate (fun fo => g.prods.flatMap fun (y, rhs) => (splits rhs).flatMap
    fun (_, s, post) => match s with
      | .t _ => []
      | .n x => (firstSeqB nu fi post).map (fun a => (x, some a)) ++
          (if nullSeqB nu post then (followOf fo y).map fun b => (x, b) else [])) fuel
    [(g.start, none)]
  (nu, fi, fo)

/-- The grammar is LL(1). -/
def ll1 (g : Grammar T N) : Bool :=
  let (nu, fi, fo) := compute g
  check g nu fi fo

/-! ## Soundness -/

section Sound
variable {g : Grammar T N} {nu : List N} {fi : List (N × T)} {fo : List (N × Option T)}

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem nullSeqB_map (xs : List N) :
    nullSeqB nu (xs.map (Sym.n (T := T))) = xs.all (nu.contains ·) := by
  induction xs with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons x xs ih =>
    simp only [nullSeqB, List.map_cons, List.all_cons, symNullB] at ih ⊢
    rw [ih]

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem nullable_sound (h : nuClosed g nu = true) {x : N} (hx : Nullable g x) : x ∈ nu := by
  induction hx with
  | @intro x xs hp _ ih =>
    have hall : nullSeqB nu (xs.map (Sym.n (T := T))) = true := by
      rw [nullSeqB_map]; simpa using ih
    have := List.all_eq_true.mp h _ hp
    simp only [hall, Bool.not_true, Bool.false_or] at this
    simpa using this

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem nullSeq_sound (h : nuClosed g nu = true) {α : List (Sym T N)} (hα : NullableSeq g α) :
    nullSeqB nu α = true := by
  obtain ⟨xs, rfl, hxs⟩ := hα
  rw [nullSeqB_map]
  simpa using fun y hy => nullable_sound h (hxs y hy)

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem firstSeqB_append (pre rest : List (Sym T N)) (hpre : nullSeqB nu pre = true) {a : T}
    (ha : a ∈ firstSeqB nu fi rest) : a ∈ firstSeqB nu fi (pre ++ rest) := by
  induction pre with
  | nil => simpa using ha
  | cons s pre ih =>
    cases s with
    | t b => simp [nullSeqB, symNullB] at hpre
    | n y =>
      simp only [nullSeqB, List.all_cons, symNullB, Bool.and_eq_true] at hpre
      simp only [List.cons_append, firstSeqB, hpre.1, ↓reduceIte, List.mem_append]
      exact Or.inr (ih hpre.2)

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem mem_firstOf {x : N} {a : T} (h : (x, a) ∈ fi) : a ∈ firstOf fi x := by
  simp only [firstOf, List.mem_filterMap]
  exact ⟨(x, a), h, by simp⟩

theorem first_sound (hnu : nuClosed g nu = true) (hfi : fiClosed g nu fi = true) {x : N} {a : T}
    (hx : First g x a) : (x, a) ∈ fi := by
  induction hx with
  | @term x pre post a hp hpre =>
    have ha : a ∈ firstSeqB nu fi (pre ++ Sym.t a :: post) :=
      firstSeqB_append pre _ (nullSeq_sound hnu hpre) (by simp [firstSeqB])
    have := List.all_eq_true.mp hfi _ hp
    simpa using List.all_eq_true.mp this a ha
  | @nonterm x y pre post a hp hpre _ ih =>
    have ha : a ∈ firstSeqB nu fi (pre ++ Sym.n y :: post) :=
      firstSeqB_append pre _ (nullSeq_sound hnu hpre)
        (by simp only [firstSeqB, List.mem_append]; exact Or.inl (mem_firstOf ih))
    have := List.all_eq_true.mp hfi _ hp
    simpa using List.all_eq_true.mp this a ha

theorem firstSeq_sound (hnu : nuClosed g nu = true) (hfi : fiClosed g nu fi = true)
    {α : List (Sym T N)} {a : T} (h : FirstSeq g α a) : a ∈ firstSeqB nu fi α := by
  obtain ⟨pre, post, hpre, hα⟩ := h
  rcases hα with rfl | ⟨y, rfl, hy⟩
  · exact firstSeqB_append pre _ (nullSeq_sound hnu hpre) (by simp [firstSeqB])
  · exact firstSeqB_append pre _ (nullSeq_sound hnu hpre)
      (by simp only [firstSeqB, List.mem_append]; exact Or.inl (mem_firstOf (first_sound hnu hfi hy)))

omit [DecidableEq T] [DecidableEq N] in
theorem mem_splits (pre post : List (Sym T N)) (s : Sym T N) :
    (pre, s, post) ∈ splits (pre ++ s :: post) := by
  induction pre with
  | nil => simp [splits]
  | cons p pre ih =>
    simp only [List.cons_append, splits, List.mem_cons, List.mem_map]
    exact Or.inr ⟨(pre, s, post), ih, rfl⟩

omit [DecidableEq T] in
theorem mem_followOf {x : N} {b : Option T} (h : (x, b) ∈ fo) : b ∈ followOf fo x := by
  simp only [followOf, List.mem_filterMap]
  exact ⟨(x, b), h, by simp⟩

theorem follow_sound (hnu : nuClosed g nu = true) (hfi : fiClosed g nu fi = true)
    (hfo : foClosed g nu fi fo = true) {x : N} {b : Option T} (h : Follow g x b) : (x, b) ∈ fo := by
  simp only [foClosed, Bool.and_eq_true] at hfo
  obtain ⟨hstart, hrules⟩ := hfo
  induction h with
  | start => simpa using hstart
  | @first y x pre post a hp ha =>
    have hr := List.all_eq_true.mp (List.all_eq_true.mp hrules _ hp) _ (mem_splits pre post _)
    simp only [Bool.and_eq_true] at hr
    simpa using List.all_eq_true.mp hr.1 a (firstSeq_sound hnu hfi ha)
  | @tail y x pre post b hp hpost _ ih =>
    have hr := List.all_eq_true.mp (List.all_eq_true.mp hrules _ hp) _ (mem_splits pre post _)
    simp only [Bool.and_eq_true, nullSeq_sound hnu hpost, Bool.not_true, Bool.false_or] at hr
    simpa using List.all_eq_true.mp hr.2 b (mem_followOf ih)

theorem predicts_sound (hnu : nuClosed g nu = true) (hfi : fiClosed g nu fi = true)
    (hfo : foClosed g nu fi fo = true) {i : Nat} {b : Option T} {x : N} {rhs : List (Sym T N)}
    (hi : g.prods[i]? = some (x, rhs)) (h : Predicts g i b) : b ∈ predictB nu fi fo x rhs := by
  obtain ⟨x', rhs', hi', hp⟩ := h
  rw [hi] at hi'
  cases hi'
  simp only [predictB, List.mem_append, List.mem_map]
  rcases hp with ⟨a, rfl, ha⟩ | ⟨hn, hf⟩
  · exact Or.inl ⟨a, firstSeq_sound hnu hfi ha, rfl⟩
  · exact Or.inr (by simp only [nullSeq_sound hnu hn, ↓reduceIte]; exact mem_followOf (follow_sound hnu hfi hfo hf))

/-- **Soundness of the check.** If `check` accepts the sets, two productions of one nonterminal
predicted on the same lookahead are the same production. -/
theorem check_sound (h : check g nu fi fo = true) {i j : Nat} {x : N} {b : Option T}
    (hi : (g.prods[i]?.map Prod.fst) = some x) (hj : (g.prods[j]?.map Prod.fst) = some x)
    (pi : Predicts g i b) (pj : Predicts g j b) : i = j := by
  simp only [check, Bool.and_eq_true] at h
  obtain ⟨⟨⟨hnu, hfi⟩, hfo⟩, hcf⟩ := h
  cases hgi : g.prods[i]? with
  | none => simp [hgi] at hi
  | some pr =>
  cases hgj : g.prods[j]? with
  | none => simp [hgj] at hj
  | some ps =>
  obtain ⟨xi, ri⟩ := pr
  obtain ⟨xj, rj⟩ := ps
  simp only [hgi, hgj, Option.map_some, Option.some.injEq] at hi hj
  subst hi hj
  have bi := predicts_sound hnu hfi hfo hgi pi
  have bj := predicts_sound hnu hfi hfo hgj pj
  have hil : i < g.prods.length := by
    rcases Nat.lt_or_ge i g.prods.length with h | h
    · exact h
    · simp [List.getElem?_eq_none h] at hgi
  have hjl : j < g.prods.length := by
    rcases Nat.lt_or_ge j g.prods.length with h | h
    · exact h
    · simp [List.getElem?_eq_none h] at hgj
  have hc := List.all_eq_true.mp (List.all_eq_true.mp hcf i (List.mem_range.mpr hil)) j
    (List.mem_range.mpr hjl)
  simp only [hgi, hgj, bne_self_eq_false, Bool.false_or, Bool.or_eq_true, beq_iff_eq] at hc
  rcases hc with hc | hc
  · exact hc
  · have := List.all_eq_true.mp hc b bi
    simp [bj] at this

/-- **LL(1) soundness.** If `ll1 g = true`, no lookahead predicts two different productions of one
nonterminal. This is the conflict-freedom part of a parser proof. The parser's own proof adds: the
lemmas linking nullable/FIRST/FOLLOW to the valid next production, that its table is built from
`predictB` (whose disjointness `check` gives directly), and its stack, matching, end-of-input and
termination invariants. Acceptance does not certify that every nonterminal is defined or
productive. -/
theorem ll1_sound (h : ll1 g = true) {i j : Nat} {x : N} {b : Option T}
    (hi : (g.prods[i]?.map Prod.fst) = some x) (hj : (g.prods[j]?.map Prod.fst) = some x)
    (pi : Predicts g i b) (pj : Predicts g j b) : i = j :=
  check_sound h hi hj pi pj

end Sound

/-! ## Examples -/

namespace Examples

inductive Tm where
  | num | plus | lparen | rparen
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Nt where
  | expr | rest | atom
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

open Sym in
/-- `expr → atom rest`, `rest → + atom rest | ε`, `atom → num | ( expr )`: LL(1). -/
def sums : Grammar Tm Nt :=
  { start := .expr,
    prods := [(.expr, [n .atom, n .rest]),
              (.rest, [t .plus, n .atom, n .rest]), (.rest, []),
              (.atom, [t .num]), (.atom, [t .lparen, n .expr, t .rparen])] }

example : ll1 sums = true := by decide

open Sym in
/-- `expr → atom + expr | atom`: both alternatives start with `atom`, so not LL(1). -/
def unfactored : Grammar Tm Nt :=
  { start := .expr,
    prods := [(.expr, [n .atom, t .plus, n .expr]), (.expr, [n .atom]),
              (.atom, [t .num]), (.atom, [t .lparen, n .expr, t .rparen])] }

example : ll1 unfactored = false := by decide

open Sym in
/-- A FIRST/FOLLOW conflict: `rest → + atom | ε` where `+` can also follow `rest`. -/
def followConflict : Grammar Tm Nt :=
  { start := .expr,
    prods := [(.expr, [n .rest, t .plus, n .atom]),
              (.rest, [t .plus, n .atom]), (.rest, []),
              (.atom, [t .num])] }

example : ll1 followConflict = false := by decide

end Examples

end Concrete.LL1
import ConcreteSpec.Spec.Slice1Semantics

/-!
# BIR: the backend IR, the contract both backends read

PROVISIONAL (slice 0; see `Concrete/README.md`, "BIR is a complete backend contract").

BIR is the one boundary that the LLVM printer and the LCB adapter read. It is block-argument SSA over
the spec's integer types (`ConcreteSpec.IntTy`, all eight widths and signednesses, so that the
integer slices extend it without reshaping) and `bool` flags. Integer values are mathematical
integers inside their type's range (`IntTy.inRange`), as in the spec; each backend chooses its bit
representation.

* `bin op t d a b` is the wrapping operation (the result reduced into `t`'s range);
* `binOvf op t d f a b` also sets the flag `f` exactly when the mathematical result is outside `t`;
* `trap s` ends the run at trap site `s`, an index into the program's site table, which gives the
  trap's kind, its typed source site and its span.

A run ends by returning a `u8` from `main`'s body, by trapping, or by getting stuck (only possible
for a program that is not well formed). `Behaviour` is the fuel-free meaning; `encode`/`decode` is
the result word every backend's body returns, decoded by the runtime entry.
-/

namespace Concrete.BIR
open ConcreteSpec (IntTy Trap)

/-! ## Sites -/

/-- A typed source-site identity, minted by the frontend. -/
structure SiteId where
  id : Nat
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A byte span in the source. -/
structure Span where
  start : Nat
  stop : Nat
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A source location as the trap line reports it: the path relative to the project root with
forward slashes, and a 1-based line and column. -/
structure Loc where
  path : String
  line : Nat
  col : Nat
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A character that may appear in the trap line's path: not a C0 or C1 control, DEL, or a
Unicode line or paragraph separator, so the line stays one line; and not `\`. -/
def pathChar (c : Char) : Bool :=
  let n := c.toNat
  n ≥ 32 && n != 127 && !(0x80 ≤ n && n ≤ 0x9F) && n != 0x2028 && n != 0x2029 && c != '\\'

/-- The `/`-separated segments of a path. -/
def segments : List Char → List (List Char)
  | [] => [[]]
  | c :: cs =>
    let rest := segments cs
    if c = '/' then [] :: rest
    else match rest with
      | seg :: more => (c :: seg) :: more
      | [] => [[c]]

/-- A location the trap line can carry: a canonical project-relative path (segments separated by
`/`, none empty, `.` or `..`, no drive prefix like `C:`) of allowed characters, and a 1-based line
and column. Resolving the project root and symlinks is the frontend's job. -/
def Loc.wf (l : Loc) : Bool :=
  let cs := l.path.toList
  let drive := match cs with
    | c :: ':' :: _ => c.isAlpha
    | _ => false
  cs.all pathChar && !drive &&
  (segments cs).all (fun seg => !seg.isEmpty && seg != ['.'] && seg != ['.', '.']) &&
  l.line ≥ 1 && l.col ≥ 1

/-- One trap site: what traps there, where it came from. -/
structure SiteEntry where
  kind : Trap
  site : SiteId
  span : Span
  loc : Loc
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

abbrev SiteTable := List SiteEntry

/-! ## Syntax -/

/-- BIR value types. -/
inductive Ty where
  | int (t : IntTy)
  | bool
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

abbrev Reg := Nat

/-- An operand: a register, or an integer immediate of a type. -/
inductive Operand where
  | reg (r : Reg)
  | imm (t : IntTy) (v : Int)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The arithmetic operations. Slice 0 uses `add`; the others are here so the integer slices
extend BIR without reshaping it. -/
inductive BinOp where
  | add | sub | mul
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Inst where
  /-- `d := wrap_t (a op b)`. Emitted only where a discharged-evidence token shows the result is
  in range, or where the source asks for wrapping. -/
  | bin (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (d : Reg) (a b : Operand)
  /-- `d := wrap_t (a op b)` and `f := (a op b) ∉ t`. -/
  | binOvf (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (d f : Reg) (a b : Operand)
  /-- `d := wrap_to a` for `a` of type `from`: the decided bit-discarding `truncate`, and the
  widening conversions, which keep the value. -/
  | trunc («from» to : IntTy) (d : Reg) (a : Operand)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Term where
  /-- Return the `u8` operand from `main`'s body. -/
  | ret (a : Operand)
  | br (target : Nat) (args : List Operand)
  | condbr (c : Reg) (yes : Nat) (yesArgs : List Operand) (no : Nat) (noArgs : List Operand)
  /-- Trap at site `s` (an index into the program's site table). -/
  | trap (s : Nat)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

structure Block where
  params : List (Reg × Ty)
  insts : List Inst
  term : Term
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- `main`'s body: blocks (block 0 is the entry) and the trap-site table. -/
structure Program where
  blocks : List Block
  sites : SiteTable
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-! ## Values and operations -/

inductive Val where
  | int (t : IntTy) (v : Int)
  | bool (b : Bool)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The mathematical result of an operation. -/
def BinOp.exact : BinOp → Int → Int → Int
  | .add, a, b => a + b
  | .sub, a, b => a - b
  | .mul, a, b => a * b

/-- `n` reduced into `t`'s range, modulo `2^bits` (two's complement for a signed type). -/
def wrap (t : IntTy) (n : Int) : Int := (n - t.min) % ((2 : Int) ^ t.bits) + t.min

abbrev Env := List (Reg × Val)

def lookup (env : Env) (r : Reg) : Option Val :=
  match env with
  | [] => none
  | (r', v) :: rest => if r' = r then some v else lookup rest r

def evalOp (env : Env) : Operand → Option Val
  | .reg r => lookup env r
  | .imm t v => if t.inRange v then some (.int t v) else none

/-- Two integer operands of type `t`. -/
def intArgs (env : Env) (t : IntTy) (a b : Operand) : Option (Int × Int) :=
  match evalOp env a, evalOp env b with
  | some (.int ta x), some (.int tb y) => if ta = t ∧ tb = t then some (x, y) else none
  | _, _ => none

def execInst (env : Env) : Inst → Option Env
  | .bin op t d a b => do
    let (x, y) ← intArgs env t a b
    pure ((d, .int t (wrap t (op.exact x y))) :: env)
  | .binOvf op t d f a b => do
    let (x, y) ← intArgs env t a b
    let r := op.exact x y
    pure ((f, .bool (!t.inRange r)) :: (d, .int t (wrap t r)) :: env)
  | .trunc fr to d a => match evalOp env a with
    | some (.int t x) => if t = fr then some ((d, .int to (wrap to x)) :: env) else none
    | _ => none

def execInsts (env : Env) : List Inst → Option Env
  | [] => some env
  | i :: is => match execInst env i with
    | some env' => execInsts env' is
    | none => none

/-- Enter block `target` with arguments evaluated in `env` (all read before any is bound): the block
exists, the arity matches, and its parameters are bound in front of `env`. -/
def enter (p : Program) (target : Nat) (args : List Operand) (env : Env) : Option Env := do
  let blk ← p.blocks[target]?
  if blk.params.length ≠ args.length then none
  let vals ← args.mapM (evalOp env)
  pure ((blk.params.map Prod.fst).zip vals ++ env)

/-! ## Semantics -/

/-- How a run ends, or that the fuel ran out. -/
inductive Outcome where
  | exited (c : Nat)
  | trapped (s : Nat)
  | stuck
  | outOfFuel
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def Outcome.final : Outcome → Bool
  | .outOfFuel => false
  | _ => true

/-- Run block `j` and what follows, with `fuel` block executions. Blocks are named by index, as
in LCB's state. -/
def runBlock (p : Program) : Nat → Nat → Env → Outcome
  | 0, _, _ => .outOfFuel
  | fuel + 1, j, env =>
    match p.blocks[j]? with
    | none => .stuck
    | some blk =>
    match execInsts env blk.insts with
    | none => .stuck
    | some env' =>
      match blk.term with
      | .ret a => match evalOp env' a with
        | some (.int .u8 c) => .exited c.toNat
        | _ => .stuck
      | .trap s => if s < p.sites.length then .trapped s else .stuck
      | .br t args => match enter p t args env' with
        | some env'' => runBlock p fuel t env''
        | none => .stuck
      | .condbr c t ta e ea => match lookup env' c with
        | some (.bool true) => match enter p t ta env' with
          | some env'' => runBlock p fuel t env''
          | none => .stuck
        | some (.bool false) => match enter p e ea env' with
          | some env'' => runBlock p fuel e env''
          | none => .stuck
        | _ => .stuck

/-- Run `main`'s body (block 0, empty environment) with `fuel` block executions. -/
def run (p : Program) (fuel : Nat) : Outcome :=
  match p.blocks[0]? with
  | some _ => runBlock p fuel 0 []
  | none => .stuck

/-- More fuel never changes a final outcome. -/
theorem runBlock_mono (p : Program) :
    ∀ (n k j : Nat) (env : Env), (runBlock p n j env).final = true →
      runBlock p (n + k) j env = runBlock p n j env := by
  intro n
  induction n with
  | zero => intro k j env h; simp [runBlock, Outcome.final] at h
  | succ n ih =>
    intro k j env h
    rw [show n + 1 + k = (n + k) + 1 by omega]
    simp only [runBlock] at h ⊢
    cases hb : p.blocks[j]? with
    | none => rfl
    | some blk =>
    simp only [hb] at h ⊢
    cases he : execInsts env blk.insts with
    | none => rfl
    | some env' =>
    simp only [he] at h ⊢
    cases ht : blk.term with
    | ret a => rfl
    | trap s => rfl
    | br t args =>
      simp only [ht] at h ⊢
      cases hn : enter p t args env' with
      | none => rfl
      | some env'' => simp only [hn] at h ⊢; exact ih k t env'' h
    | condbr c t ta e ea =>
      simp only [ht] at h ⊢
      cases hc : lookup env' c with
      | none => rfl
      | some v =>
        cases v with
        | int _ _ => rfl
        | bool b =>
          cases b with
          | true =>
            simp only [hc] at h ⊢
            cases hn : enter p t ta env' with
            | none => rfl
            | some env'' => simp only [hn] at h ⊢; exact ih k t env'' h
          | false =>
            simp only [hc] at h ⊢
            cases hn : enter p e ea env' with
            | none => rfl
            | some env'' => simp only [hn] at h ⊢; exact ih k e env'' h

theorem run_mono {p : Program} {n m : Nat} (h : (run p n).final = true) (hm : n ≤ m) :
    run p m = run p n := by
  obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hm
  unfold run at h ⊢
  split
  · rename_i hb; simp only [hb] at h; exact runBlock_mono p n k 0 [] h
  · rfl

/-- Two final outcomes of the same program agree. -/
theorem run_final_unique {p : Program} {n m : Nat}
    (hn : (run p n).final = true) (hm : (run p m).final = true) : run p n = run p m := by
  rw [← run_mono hn (Nat.le_max_left n m), ← run_mono hm (Nat.le_max_right n m)]

/-! ## Behaviour: the fuel-free meaning -/

/-- A run's behaviour as the parent process sees it, up to the trap's kind. `refused` (a startup
refusal) and `killed` arise only for native runs. -/
inductive Behaviour where
  | exited (c : Nat)
  | trapped (k : Trap)
  | diverges
  | stuck
  | refused
  | killed
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def Outcome.toBehaviour (tbl : SiteTable) : Outcome → Behaviour
  | .exited c => .exited c
  | .trapped s => match tbl[s]? with
    | some e => .trapped e.kind
    | none => .stuck
  | .stuck => .stuck
  | .outOfFuel => .diverges

open Classical in
/-- The behaviour: the final outcome if some fuel reaches one, otherwise divergence. -/
noncomputable def behaviour (p : Program) : Behaviour :=
  if h : ∃ n, (run p n).final = true then (run p (Classical.choose h)).toBehaviour p.sites
  else .diverges

theorem behaviour_of_run {p : Program} {n : Nat} (h : (run p n).final = true) :
    behaviour p = (run p n).toBehaviour p.sites := by
  have hex : ∃ n, (run p n).final = true := ⟨n, h⟩
  unfold behaviour
  split
  · rename_i h'
    rw [run_final_unique (Classical.choose_spec h') h]
  · exact absurd hex ‹_›

/-! ## The result word

Every backend emits `main`'s body as a function returning a 32-bit word that encodes how the run
ended; one runtime entry decodes it (`Concrete/Backend/Runtime.lean`). Exiting with `c` returns
`c`; trapping at site `s` returns `256 + s`. The site table gives the trap's kind and site, so a
trap is never confused with `return 134`. -/

/-- The largest site table the encoding supports. -/
def maxSites : Nat := 2 ^ 31 - 256

/-- The word a final outcome encodes to. -/
def encode : Outcome → Option Nat
  | .exited c => some c
  | .trapped s => some (256 + s)
  | _ => none

/-- An outcome the encoding represents: an exit status below 256, or a trap at a site of the
table. -/
def Outcome.Encodable (tbl : SiteTable) : Outcome → Prop
  | .exited c => c < 256
  | .trapped s => s < tbl.length ∧ tbl.length ≤ maxSites
  | _ => False

/-- The runtime's reading of a result word. A word that encodes nothing is `stuck`. -/
def decode (tbl : SiteTable) (w : BitVec 32) : Behaviour :=
  if w.toNat < 256 then .exited w.toNat
  else match tbl[w.toNat - 256]? with
    | some e => .trapped e.kind
    | none => .stuck

/-- The round trip: decoding the word an outcome encodes to gives the outcome's behaviour. -/
theorem decode_encode (tbl : SiteTable) (o : Outcome) (n : Nat) (he : encode o = some n)
    (hv : o.Encodable tbl) : decode tbl (BitVec.ofNat 32 n) = o.toBehaviour tbl := by
  cases o with
  | exited c =>
    simp only [encode, Option.some.injEq] at he
    subst he
    simp only [Outcome.Encodable] at hv
    have h32 : c < 2 ^ 32 := by omega
    simp [decode, Outcome.toBehaviour, BitVec.toNat_ofNat, Nat.mod_eq_of_lt h32, hv]
  | trapped s =>
    simp only [encode, Option.some.injEq] at he
    subst he
    simp only [Outcome.Encodable, maxSites] at hv
    have h32 : 256 + s < 2 ^ 32 := by omega
    have hn : ¬ 256 + s < 256 := by omega
    simp [decode, Outcome.toBehaviour, BitVec.toNat_ofNat, Nat.mod_eq_of_lt h32, hn]
  | stuck => simp [encode] at he
  | outOfFuel => simp [encode] at he

/-- What a backend's body function produces. -/
inductive BodyResult where
  | returns (w : BitVec 32)
  | diverges
  | stuck
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def BodyResult.behaviour (tbl : SiteTable) : BodyResult → Behaviour
  | .returns w => decode tbl w
  | .diverges => .diverges
  | .stuck => .stuck

/-! ## The overflow-checked operations -/

/-- `binOvf` computes the wrapped result and flags exactly the out-of-range results, for every
operation, every integer type and all in-range operands: the check is total, not an
approximation. -/
theorem binOvf_spec (env : Env) (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (d f : Reg) (a b : Operand) (x y : Int)
    (h : intArgs env t a b = some (x, y)) :
    execInst env (.binOvf op t d f a b) =
      some ((f, .bool (!t.inRange (op.exact x y))) :: (d, .int t (wrap t (op.exact x y))) :: env) := by
  simp [execInst, h]

/-- Wrapping leaves an in-range result unchanged. -/
theorem wrap_of_inRange (t : IntTy) (n : Int) (h : t.inRange n = true) : wrap t n = n := by
  cases t <;>
    simp_all [wrap, IntTy.inRange, IntTy.min, IntTy.max, IntTy.signed, IntTy.bits] <;>
    omega

/-- The wrapped result is always in range. -/
theorem wrap_inRange (t : IntTy) (n : Int) : t.inRange (wrap t n) = true := by
  cases t <;>
    simp [wrap, IntTy.inRange, IntTy.min, IntTy.max, IntTy.signed, IntTy.bits] <;>
    omega

/-! ## Well-formedness

A decidable check of the program's shape. Values cross blocks only as block arguments: an operand
must be defined EARLIER IN ITS OWN BLOCK (a block parameter or an earlier instruction's result), so
every use is dominated by its definition by construction (what LLVM's verifier demands), and a
well-formed program never reads an undefined register (`run_progress`, in
`Concrete/BIR/Progress.lean`). Beyond that: types, arities, targets (never the entry), every non-entry block
has a predecessor, trap sites exist, and every register is defined once. -/

def lookupTy (types : List (Reg × Ty)) (r : Reg) : Option Ty :=
  match types with
  | [] => none
  | (r', t) :: rest => if r' = r then some t else lookupTy rest r

def Inst.defs : Inst → List (Reg × Ty)
  | .bin _ t d _ _ => [(d, .int t)]
  | .binOvf _ t d f _ _ => [(d, .int t), (f, .bool)]
  | .trunc _ to d _ => [(d, .int to)]

def Block.defs (b : Block) : List (Reg × Ty) := b.params ++ b.insts.flatMap Inst.defs

/-- Every register the program defines, with its type. -/
def Program.defs (p : Program) : List (Reg × Ty) := p.blocks.flatMap Block.defs

/-- The frame-accounting handoff: how many registers the body defines. Native stack bytes never
come from this number (they come from the final artifact and the launcher's reservation). -/
def Program.frameSlots (p : Program) : Nat := p.defs.length

/-- `a` has type `ty` in `scope` (the registers defined so far in the block). -/
def operandHas (scope : List (Reg × Ty)) (ty : Ty) : Operand → Bool
  | .reg r => lookupTy scope r == some ty
  | .imm t v => ty == .int t && t.inRange v

def Inst.wf (scope : List (Reg × Ty)) : Inst → Bool
  | .bin _ t _ a b => operandHas scope (.int t) a && operandHas scope (.int t) b
  | .binOvf _ t _ _ a b => operandHas scope (.int t) a && operandHas scope (.int t) b
  | .trunc fr _ _ a => operandHas scope (.int fr) a

/-- A branch to `target` with `args`: the block exists, is not the entry, and the arguments
match its parameters' types. -/
def edgeWf (p : Program) (scope : List (Reg × Ty)) (target : Nat) (args : List Operand) : Bool :=
  target != 0 &&
  match p.blocks[target]? with
  | none => false
  | some blk => blk.params.length == args.length &&
      (blk.params.zip args).all (fun (pr, a) => operandHas scope pr.2 a)

def Term.wf (p : Program) (scope : List (Reg × Ty)) : Term → Bool
  | .ret a => operandHas scope (.int .u8) a
  | .br t args => edgeWf p scope t args
  | .condbr c t ta e ea =>
    lookupTy scope c == some .bool && t != e && edgeWf p scope t ta && edgeWf p scope e ea
  | .trap s => s < p.sites.length

/-- A block: each instruction's operands are defined earlier in the block. -/
def Block.wf (p : Program) (b : Block) : Bool :=
  let (ok, scope) := b.insts.foldl
    (fun (acc : Bool × List (Reg × Ty)) i => (acc.1 && Inst.wf acc.2 i, i.defs.reverse ++ acc.2))
    (true, b.params.reverse)
  ok && b.term.wf p scope

def Term.targets : Term → List Nat
  | .br t _ => [t]
  | .condbr _ t _ e _ => [t, e]
  | _ => []

def wf (p : Program) : Bool :=
  let regs := p.defs.map Prod.fst
  (match p.blocks[0]? with | some b => b.params.isEmpty | none => false) &&
  regs.eraseDups.length == regs.length &&
  p.sites.length ≤ maxSites && p.sites.all (·.loc.wf) &&
  (List.range p.blocks.length).all
    (fun j => j == 0 || p.blocks.any (fun b => b.term.targets.contains j)) &&
  p.blocks.all (Block.wf p)

/-- A well-formed BIR program. -/
def WF (p : Program) : Prop := wf p = true

instance (p : Program) : Decidable (WF p) := inferInstanceAs (Decidable (wf p = true))

/-! ## Hand-written examples (slice 0) -/

namespace Examples

/-- The one trap site of the examples: the checked `+`. -/
def plusSite : SiteEntry := ⟨.arithmeticOverflow, ⟨0⟩, ⟨59, 64⟩, ⟨"tests/slice0/sum.con", 3, 12⟩⟩

/-- `a + b` with the overflow check: `binOvf`, then branch to the trap site or return. -/
def checkedSum (a b : Int) : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [.binOvf .add .u8 0 1 (.imm .u8 a) (.imm .u8 b)],
        term := .condbr 1 1 [] 2 [.reg 0] },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .trap 0 },
      { params := [(2, .int .u8)], insts := [], term := .ret (.reg 2) }],
    sites := [plusSite] }

/-- `20 + 22` exits with 42. -/
def answer : Program := checkedSum 20 22
/-- `200 + 55` exits with 255, the boundary. -/
def boundary : Program := checkedSum 200 55
/-- `200 + 56` traps at the `+` (as BIR: the checker refuses this source, but BIR must still trap). -/
def overflow : Program := checkedSum 200 56

/-- The discharged form: a `+` whose evidence token shows it cannot overflow has no check. -/
def answerDischarged : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [.bin .add .u8 0 (.imm .u8 20) (.imm .u8 22)],
        term := .ret (.reg 0) }],
    sites := [] }

/-- `return 134` exits with 134, which the result word keeps apart from a trap. -/
def return134 : Program :=
  { blocks := [{ params := [], insts := [], term := .ret (.imm .u8 134) }], sites := [] }

example : WF answer := by decide +kernel
example : WF overflow := by decide +kernel
example : WF answerDischarged := by decide
example : WF return134 := by decide
example : run answer 3 = .exited 42 := by decide
example : run boundary 3 = .exited 255 := by decide
example : run overflow 3 = .trapped 0 := by decide
example : run answerDischarged 1 = .exited 42 := by decide
example : run return134 1 = .exited 134 := by decide
example : encode (.trapped 0) = some 256 := rfl

/-- `a op b` at type `t` with the overflow check, exiting 0 when it is in range: exercises the
other widths, signedness and operations BIR already has. -/
def checkedOp (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (a b : Int) : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [.binOvf op t 0 1 (.imm t a) (.imm t b)],
        term := .condbr 1 1 [] 2 [] },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .trap 0 },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .ret (.imm .u8 0) }],
    sites := [plusSite] }

example : run (checkedOp .add .i8 (-100) (-28)) 3 = .exited 0 := by decide
example : run (checkedOp .add .i8 (-100) (-29)) 3 = .trapped 0 := by decide

/-- Observe a wrapped result: run `pre`, then `a op b` at `t` with the overflow flag, truncate the
wrapped result to `u8`, and exit with it, plus 128 when the flag is set. A wrong mask, rebias,
result register, flag or conversion selects a different exit code. `pre` uses registers below 10. -/
def observe (pre : List Inst) (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (a b : Operand) : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := pre ++ [.binOvf op t 10 11 a b, .trunc t .u8 12 (.reg 10)],
        term := .condbr 11 1 [.reg 12] 2 [.reg 12] },
      { params := [(13, .int .u8)], insts := [.bin .add .u8 14 (.reg 13) (.imm .u8 128)],
        term := .ret (.reg 14) },
      { params := [(15, .int .u8)], insts := [], term := .ret (.reg 15) }],
    sites := [] }

def wrapped (op : BinOp) (t : IntTy) (a b : Int) : Program := observe [] op t (.imm t a) (.imm t b)

example : WF (wrapped .mul .u16 65535 65535) := by decide
example : run (wrapped .mul .u16 65535 65535) 2 = .exited 129 := by decide
example : run (wrapped .sub .i8 (-100) 100) 2 = .exited 184 := by decide

/-- Widening keeps the value: `u8 200` zero-extends to `i16 200` (overflowing `+ 32600`), and
`i8 -56` sign-extends to `i16 -56` (overflowing `+ -32750`); the other extension would not. -/
def widenU : Program := observe [.trunc .u8 .i16 0 (.imm .u8 200)] .add .i16 (.reg 0) (.imm .i16 32600)
def widenS : Program :=
  observe [.trunc .i8 .i16 0 (.imm .i8 (-56))] .add .i16 (.reg 0) (.imm .i16 (-32750))

example : run widenU 2 = .exited 160 := by decide
example : run widenS 2 = .exited 90 := by decide

/-- Two sites with ids and spans unlike their indices; `a + b` is checked at site 0, then the sum
times `c` at site 1. -/
def twoSites (a b c : Int) : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [.binOvf .add .u8 0 1 (.imm .u8 a) (.imm .u8 b)],
        term := .condbr 1 1 [] 2 [.reg 0] },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .trap 0 },
      { params := [(4, .int .u8)], insts := [.binOvf .mul .u8 2 3 (.reg 4) (.imm .u8 c)],
        term := .condbr 3 3 [] 4 [.reg 2] },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .trap 1 },
      { params := [(5, .int .u8)], insts := [], term := .ret (.reg 5) }],
    sites := [⟨.arithmeticOverflow, ⟨3⟩, ⟨10, 15⟩, ⟨"src/main.con", 1, 11⟩⟩,
              ⟨.arithmeticOverflow, ⟨9⟩, ⟨40, 47⟩, ⟨"src/ñandú \"x\"/lib.con", 17, 5⟩⟩] }

example : WF (twoSites 100 100 2) := by decide +kernel
example : run (twoSites 100 100 2) 5 = .trapped 1 := by decide
example : run (twoSites 200 100 2) 5 = .trapped 0 := by decide
example : run (twoSites 20 1 2) 5 = .exited 42 := by decide

/-! Shapes `wf` refuses: a parameter block nothing branches to (it would print a phi with no
arms), and a value used outside the block that defines it (or before its definition). -/
def unreachableParam : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .ret (.imm .u8 0) },
      { params := [(0, .int .u8)], insts := [], term := .ret (.reg 0) }],
    sites := [] }

def crossBlockUse : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [], insts := [.bin .add .u8 0 (.imm .u8 1) (.imm .u8 2)], term := .br 1 [] },
      { params := [], insts := [], term := .ret (.reg 0) }],
    sites := [] }

def useBeforeDef : Program :=
  { blocks := [
      { params := [],
        insts := [.bin .add .u8 0 (.reg 1) (.imm .u8 2), .bin .add .u8 1 (.imm .u8 1) (.imm .u8 2)],
        term := .ret (.reg 0) }],
    sites := [] }

example : Loc.wf ⟨"/abs/main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src\\main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/main.con\n", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/main.con", 0, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/main.con", 1, 0⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/\u0085main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/\u2028main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"../outside.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/../main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"./main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src//main.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"C:/outside.con", 1, 1⟩ = false := by decide +kernel
example : Loc.wf ⟨"src/ñandú \"x\"/lib.con", 17, 5⟩ = true := by decide +kernel
example : ¬ WF unreachableParam := by decide
example : ¬ WF crossBlockUse := by decide
example : ¬ WF useBeforeDef := by decide

/-- The examples, by name, with the outcome each must show (`scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_backends.sh`). -/
def all : List (String × Program × Outcome) :=
  [("answer", answer, .exited 42), ("boundary", boundary, .exited 255),
   ("overflow", overflow, .trapped 0), ("answer_discharged", answerDischarged, .exited 42),
   ("return134", return134, .exited 134),
   ("i8_add_ok", checkedOp .add .i8 (-100) (-28), .exited 0),
   ("i8_add_ovf", checkedOp .add .i8 (-100) (-29), .trapped 0),
   ("u16_mul_ok", checkedOp .mul .u16 300 218, .exited 0),
   ("u16_mul_ovf", checkedOp .mul .u16 300 219, .trapped 0),
   ("i16_sub_ovf", checkedOp .sub .i16 (-32768) 1, .trapped 0),
   ("u8_sub_ovf", checkedOp .sub .u8 0 1, .trapped 0),
   ("i8_mul_ok", checkedOp .mul .i8 (-16) 8, .exited 0),
   ("i8_mul_ovf", checkedOp .mul .i8 (-16) (-8), .trapped 0),
   ("w_i8_add", wrapped .add .i8 100 100, .exited 72),
   ("w_i8_add_ok", wrapped .add .i8 (-100) 50, .exited 206),
   ("w_i8_sub", wrapped .sub .i8 (-100) 100, .exited 184),
   ("w_i8_mul", wrapped .mul .i8 (-16) 9, .exited 240),
   ("w_u8_add", wrapped .add .u8 200 100, .exited 172),
   ("w_u8_sub", wrapped .sub .u8 3 5, .exited 126),
   ("w_u8_mul", wrapped .mul .u8 20 20, .exited 16),
   ("w_i16_add", wrapped .add .i16 32767 1, .exited 128),
